    card title "Which ramp is fastest?"

Three ramps join the same two points. Which one gets a ball to the bottom first?
    draw start finish r_straight r_curve r_drop

One is straight, one swoops down in a curve, and one drops steeply, then runs flat.
    blocking false

    highlight r_straight duration 1.5

    highlight r_curve duration 1.9

    highlight r_drop duration 2.6

    ask "Which ramp gets a rolling ball from start to finish fastest? Tap it." choose ["r_straight","r_curve","r_drop"] answer r_curve store c stage own
        right: The curve. It dips first, so the ball gains speed early, and that speed carries it the rest of the way.
        wrong: Think about where the ball picks up its speed.

    draw b1

Race them. The straight line is the shortest path, but the ball gathers speed slowly.
    move b1 path [[700,-230]] duration 3.3 easing ease-in

    draw t_straight b2

The steep drop builds speed fast, then spends it on a long flat run.
    move b2 path [[15,-110],[35,-200],[65,-226],[110,-230],[700,-230]] duration 2.5 easing ease-in

    draw t_drop b3

The curve is steep at first, then swoops through and beats them both.
    move b3 path [[0.1,-1.3],[0.5,-5.3],[1.6,-11.7],[3.8,-20.6],[7.3,-31.8],[12.5,-45],[19.6,-60.1],[28.9,-76.6],[40.5,-94.5],[54.5,-113.2],[71.2,-132.4],[90.5,-151.9],[112.4,-171.3],[136.9,-190.1],[163.9,-208.1],[193.3,-224.9],[224.8,-240.2],[258.4,-253.7],[293.6,-265.2],[330.2,-274.5],[367.9,-281.4],[406.4,-285.7],[445.3,-287.5],[484.3,-286.6],[522.9,-283.1],[561,-277],[598,-268.5],[633.7,-257.7],[667.8,-244.8],[700,-230]] duration 2.3 easing ease-in

It even dips below the finish and climbs the last stretch: the speed it gains early is worth the climb.
    draw t_curve lvl_l

That curve is a cycloid:  (@draw cyc@)the path traced by a point on the rim of a rolling wheel.

In 1696 Johann Bernoulli challenged the best mathematicians of Europe to find this fastest path.
    highlight p_curve

Isaac Newton got the problem one afternoon in January 1697, after a day at the Royal Mint. By four the next morning, he had solved it.
    highlight l_curve cyc

He sent his answer without a name. But Bernoulli knew  (@draw claw@)at once who it was: you can tell the lion by its claw.

